  "pair_order": "pair p runs baseline then candidate when p is even and candidate then baseline when p is odd, so consecutive pairs form ABBA blocks",
  "bootstrap": {
    "resamples": 10000,
    "confidence": 0.95
  },
  "descriptions": {
    "crypto": "Layer A. One fresh process per sample, pinned to one core, time-bounded loop over one operation at one size. Arms use their automatic best backend. Both arms move data from a source buffer to a separate destination buffer, so the Rust arm includes the copy its in-place API needs.",
    "packet": "Layer B. C: bench/c/packet_bench.c includes the pinned oracle twice.c with main and tun_write renamed and drives reorder_process_packet, reorder_check_timeouts and the encrypt/frame/decrypt path on seeded traces. Traces: in_order is counters 1..N; sparse_reorder swaps one random adjacent pair in a block of 8 with probability 1/4; near_full_window holds back one counter while 1023 later counters arrive, then releases it; repeated_gaps holds back one counter while 1..64 later counters arrive; repeated_resets changes the peer random value and restarts counters every 64 packets; large_payload is repeated_gaps with 65000 byte payloads; timeout_drain buffers 512 future packets, ages them past the timeout and runs the timeout check; full_packet_* add header build, HiAE encrypt, frame assembly, header parse and HiAE decrypt in front of the reorder logic. Counter and random-value generation is splitmix64 seeded from the run seed. The Rust arm must expose the same scenarios through the same CLI and JSON.",
    "tunnel": "Layer C. Real binaries in two network namespaces joined by a veth pair. One session per arm and pair: direct no-tunnel ceilings first, then the tunnel with one warm-up, then the listed steps. Each endpoint is pinned to its own core, generators to other cores.",
    "memory": "Memory. Fresh endpoint pair per session. Generators are excluded; only the two twice processes are read."
  },
